artifacts.review.json:
{
  "directions": [
    "Extend the offer-letter rules so that a sign-on bonus or equity grant whose approval value is zero is treated as not offered, and the line is left out of the letter entirely, removing the whole table row, in the same way the relocation rule removes the paragraph and its heading when the block does not apply."
  ],
  "findings": [
    {
      "confidence": "high",
      "statement": "The finished letter kept a compensation line whose approval value was zero, and the user's rejection in each case named exactly that line. In ses_01a1c4c6 the letter and reply showed a £0 sign-on bonus and the user asked for the line to be left out. In ses_01a1c542 the letter showed an equity grant of 0 shares and the user asked for that line to be dropped. In ses_01a1cca8 both lines appeared and the user asked again for both to be left out.",
      "traceIds": [
        "ses_01a1c4c6",
        "ses_01a1c542",
        "ses_01a1cca8"
      ]
    },
    {
      "confidence": "high",
      "statement": "The written rules for these letters cover date style, preserving the bold salary and the bold underlined deadline, removing conditional blocks that do not apply, converting to PDF and asking about missing fields, but say nothing about a field whose value is zero. Each session wrote its own fill script that copies the approval value into the row, so the zeros reached the letter, and each agent saw the zero lines in its own reopen check of the saved letter and kept them.",
      "traceIds": [
        "ses_01a1c4c6",
        "ses_01a1c542",
        "ses_01a1cca8"
      ]
    },
    {
      "confidence": "high",
      "statement": "The only session whose approval had a real bonus and share count, ses_01a1c5f4 with a £4,000 bonus and 800 shares, was accepted as exactly right. No feedback in any session complained about the dates, the bold formatting, the removed relocation block, the file names or the PDF conversion.",
      "traceIds": [
        "ses_01a1c5f4",
        "ses_01a1c4c6",
        "ses_01a1c542",
        "ses_01a1cca8"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "hypotheses": [
    {
      "confidence": "medium",
      "statement": "For optional compensation, a zero in the approval means the item was not offered, so the user expects the line to be omitted entirely rather than printed as £0 or 0 shares.",
      "traceIds": [
        "ses_01a1c4c6",
        "ses_01a1c542",
        "ses_01a1cca8",
        "ses_01a1c5f4"
      ],
      "contradictions": [
        "No session in this batch shows a user accepting a printed zero line, so there is no direct counterevidence, but no letter with the line omitted has been delivered either."
      ]
    },
    {
      "confidence": "medium",
      "statement": "Adding a rule that omits zero-valued optional compensation lines would stop the rejections, since the defect recurred only while the rule set was silent on zero values and each session rebuilt the fill script from the same silent instructions.",
      "traceIds": [
        "ses_01a1c4c6",
        "ses_01a1c542",
        "ses_01a1cca8"
      ],
      "contradictions": [
        "This has not been tested: no later session in the batch ran under a rule that addresses zero values."
      ]
    }
  ],
  "summary": "Four offer-letter sessions were reviewed in full and every rejection was checked against the session record. Three of the four were rejected for one repeated reason: the letter kept a compensation line whose approval value was zero, a £0 sign-on bonus in ses_01a1c4c6 and ses_01a1cca8 and an equity grant of 0 shares in ses_01a1c542 and ses_01a1cca8, and in each case the user asked for the line to be left out. The fourth session, ses_01a1c5f4, whose approval had a real bonus and share count, was accepted as exactly right, which points at the zero values rather than at the letter pipeline as a whole. The written rules for these letters cover dates, formatting, conditional blocks, PDF conversion and missing fields, but say nothing about zero-valued fields, and each session built its own fill script that copies the approval value into the row. No complaint in any session addressed dates, bold formatting, the removed relocation block, file names or the PDF conversion. One session, ses_01a1cca8, took a long path with three failed commands, all recovered before delivery, and on that path it caught and fixed a spacing defect by reading the converted PDF, something the other sessions never did. A claim that this session delivered a letter with the salary bold lost was examined against the record and withdrawn: the agent caught its own faulty check, verified the bold directly in the document and in a rendered image, and the delivered letter kept the salary bold and the deadline bold and underlined.",
  "unresolved": [
    "Whether the PDFs delivered in the three shorter sessions contained spacing defects like the one found and fixed in ses_01a1cca8; those PDFs were never opened after conversion.",
    "Whether the omission rule should extend beyond sign-on bonus and equity to other optional fields that can be zero or blank; no feedback in this batch addresses any other field.",
    "Whether removing a zero-value table row leaves the compensation table looking incomplete; no session in this batch delivered a letter with the row deleted.",
    "The rule to ask the user when the approval leaves a needed field empty was never exercised, since every approval in this batch had all fields present."
  ],
  "version": 1
}

artifacts.proposal.json:
{
  "edits": [
    {
      "evidence": [
        "ses_01a1c4c6",
        "ses_01a1c542",
        "ses_01a1cca8"
      ],
      "newText": "- When a conditional block such as `{{IF_RELOCATION}}…{{END_IF_RELOCATION}}` does not apply, delete the whole paragraph and the heading above it. Never leave an empty paragraph or a heading with nothing under it.\n- If the hiring approval gives a sign-on bonus of £0 or an equity grant of 0 shares, the item was not offered: delete that whole compensation table row instead of filling it. Never print `£0` or `0 shares` in the letter.",
      "oldText": "- When a conditional block such as `{{IF_RELOCATION}}…{{END_IF_RELOCATION}}` does not apply, delete the whole paragraph and the heading above it. Never leave an empty paragraph or a heading with nothing under it.",
      "rationale": "Three sessions were rejected because the letter printed a £0 sign-on bonus line, a 0 shares equity line, or both, and the users asked for those lines to be left out. The rules were silent on zero values, so this states that a zero-valued item was not offered and its whole table row is deleted, placed right after the conditional-block rule it mirrors."
    }
  ],
  "note": "All three proposals converged on one rule: a zero-valued sign-on bonus or equity grant means the item was not offered, so its table row is deleted. A proposed extension of the reopen-and-check step was left out because the feedback asked only for the lines to be omitted, and one cited session with positive feedback was excluded as evidence.",
  "commit_title": "Treat zero sign-on bonus and equity grants as not offered",
  "commit_body": "Adds one rule to the offer letter guidance: a sign-on bonus of £0 or an equity grant of 0 shares means the item was not offered, so its whole table row is deleted and £0 or 0 shares is never printed. In ses_01a1c4c6 the user rejected the letter because a £0 sign-on bonus line was printed and asked for the line to be left out, in ses_01a1c542 the user said the 0 shares equity line looks odd and should be dropped when there is none, and in ses_01a1cca8 the user reported both a £0 sign-on bonus and 0 shares of equity again and asked for the lines to be left out. A second proposed change that extended the reopen-and-check step was dropped because the feedback asked only for the omission and the new rule already covers it."
}
